{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791291446,"nonce":"WxyVlhoyYReRXP7OYMl/rgS6L7s=","message":"Landed in ba737c5bef51e9f4eaceb5fbd94e859724742b6e.\n\nThe shared HIR pass scalarizes immutable allocation/phi webs before any tier runs them. Roots must be local fixed-size allocations or null; stores initialize a root in its own block. Fields and nullness become phis, loads become their SSA values, and allocations/releases disappear. Escaping addresses, pointer identity, volatile accesses, overlapping fields, writes through a phi, and opaque incoming pointers are rejected. A small constructor outside a loop is exposed only when it feeds a phi whose other input is already a visible allocation. No frontend or benchmark names drive the transform.\n\nThe original operator-overload reproducer now has three floating field phis and six fadd instructions in its hot loop, without allocation or object field access. LLVM emits paired double-vector additions for two components, including the ordinary OSR helper. Normal interpreter-first tiering and LLVM promotion are retained.\n\nNUC x86_64, LLVM enabled, five alternating processes per arm, each result checked against CPython:\n- bench_op_overload: kernel median 85.040 -\u003e 19.411 ms (4.38x); wall 117.277 -\u003e 49.522 ms (2.37x). PyPy: 26.489 ms kernel, 44.697 ms wall.\n- bench_op_overload_ref: kernel 19.384 -\u003e 5.726 ms (3.39x); wall 49.582 -\u003e 31.953 ms. PyPy: 7.080 ms kernel, 26.647 ms wall.\n\nA broad constructor-inlining experiment slowed Lua tokenize by about 40%; it was narrowed before this commit. The final six-kernel Lua alternating screen keeps checksums correct and removes that large regression. Final tokenize: 166.417 -\u003e 170.902 ms kernel, 211.212 -\u003e 216.966 ms wall; disabling only scalarization gives 169.041 / 209.782 ms. No general Lua gain is claimed. The other kernels still need work.\n\nValidation: 8 compiler integration tests on Mac, exercising interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM execution plus conservative rejection, constructor effects and cross-field recurrence; 7 of these on NUC before adding the cross-field case. Existing aggregate scalarization and promoted-stub tests pass. The inliner suite passes 17 tests. Final release conformance with LLVM: Lua 43 tests (official cases filtered), Python 50 tests; the new class fixture covers zero/one/hot loops, a retained alias and field mutation. A complete NUC PyPy-kernel fast screen completes with supported kernels verified; pidigits remains unsupported. Reports: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-heap-ab.json, /tmp/zyntax-nuc-lua-heap-ab.json, /tmp/zypy-nuc-heap-suite.json on NUC.\n\nThis fixes the reported local-allocation reproducer. Arbitrary opaque incoming objects and rematerialization on escaping branches remain outside this conservative transform.","files":null}]}